---
/**
 * /about — A2 "Named-role sections", Figma `238:101` on page
 * `Page — About (audience-first)` (`238:98`), file `wJ9DK6ByFUN6rWe0CpCVPU`.
 * Motion: `Motion — About page` (`245:2`, frame `245:3`).
 *
 * WHY A2 AND NOT A PORT. The owner's brief was "adopt similar messaging like
 * whom we build this for". A2 is the most literal answer available: it names
 * four readers, gives them four chips that double as a page map, and then
 * addresses one section to each of them, every section carrying a quantified
 * artefact cited to the statute that binds THAT reader. Cover the prose and the
 * four artefacts still make the argument, which is the test in
 * specs/DESIGN-DECISIONS.md section 3.
 *
 * NAME COLLISION, recorded so nobody re-derives it: a different Figma page
 * `Page — About` (`106:2`) holds two earlier section studies also labelled A1
 * and A2. Those are NOT this design.
 *
 * ── THE COPY RULES, AND WHY EACH ONE MATTERS ────────────────────────────────
 *
 * 1. NOBODY CLAIMS THIS WAS BUILT BY BIDDERS. Owner report O-07: "dont over
 *    claim as created by bidder but mentioned build with advise of and
 *    consulting experience and engineering background something not so over".
 *    "Built by bid practitioners" stood three times, in the hero standfirst, in
 *    an h2 and in the meta description; all three are gone. What the page says
 *    instead is the approved framing from specs/ABOUT-PAGE-PROPOSAL.md:
 *    engineers build it, and people who have written and won bids advise them.
 *    "Consultants" is still never used about US, because the Vision below
 *    promises a supplier can answer a tender "without paying a bid-writing
 *    consultancy" and naming ourselves as the thing the reader is avoiding is
 *    an own goal. The advisers' background is described; ours is not claimed.
 * 2. EACH THING IS SAID ONCE. The citation rule was stated eight times, s.52
 *    and s.71 ten times between them, the company number three times and the
 *    data residency twice. The rule is now stated in full once, in the section
 *    addressed to the reader whose job it is (role-sign), and referred to
 *    elsewhere rather than restated; the two duties are recited in full once,
 *    in "How it is built". Every other appearance is a citation on an artefact,
 *    which is evidence rather than repetition.
 * 3. THREE CLAIMS ARE DELIBERATELY UNTOUCHED AND ARE WITH THE OWNER:
 *    "multi-million pound", "The founders" plural with nobody named, and
 *    "inside regulated industries". Each now appears exactly ONCE, in the
 *    timeline, in its original wording. They are not softened here, because
 *    quietly softening a claim we cannot evidence is the same defect as making
 *    it: this site's position is that it refuses figures it cannot trace. No
 *    customer, client or project is named or implied anywhere on the page.
 *    Recorded in OWNER-REGISTER.md and status/issues.json O-07.
 * 4. Values states the refusal outright: we do not quote win rates, ours or
 *    yours. Refusing that claim is the position, and a position has to be
 *    stated to count.
 * 5. Market-neutral framing. The eyebrow no longer leads with jurisdiction,
 *    neither Mission nor Vision is scoped to the UK, and the hero standfirst no
 *    longer opens "CrowAgent Ltd is a UK company" (OA-33, the last survivor of
 *    that framing on this page). UK public is the proof point, not the identity.
 * 6. The timeline marks its unshipped item "Planned, not yet built" as TEXT.
 *    The legacy page carried that state in `opacity-50` alone, so read by
 *    anyone who cannot see the dimming a planned feature read exactly like a
 *    delivered one.
 * 7. Company facts exact, and the trust wording verbatim from data/footer.ts,
 *    including "We follow ISO 27001 controls. We are not certified yet." We
 *    hold neither SOC 2 nor ISO 27001 and must not imply otherwise.
 *
 * ── THREE DELIBERATE DEPARTURES FROM THE RENDER, EACH ARGUED ────────────────
 *
 * A. THE MARK COLOURS. The render paints the four artefact dots teal, cyan,
 *    orchid, teal. The binding palette rule (PLATFORM-CHARTER.md, and
 *    DESIGN-DECISIONS.md "Colour carries meaning") is teal = verified,
 *    violet/orchid = refused or flagged, cyan = INTERACTIVE ONLY, and it warns
 *    in terms that using all three decoratively is the generic-AI-startup
 *    palette. Read semantically, three of the four artefacts are verified facts
 *    cited to statute and exactly one is the refusal rule ("Every figure, or an
 *    open gap"). So the marks are three teal and one orchid, on the refusal.
 *    That is also the same grammar the trust row uses five rows later, five
 *    teal and one orchid on the thing we do not hold, so the page teaches the
 *    mark once and reuses it rather than spending three hues on decoration.
 *
 * B. THE STATUTE ATTRIBUTION IN ROLE 1. The render's paragraph reads "under
 *    s.71 you assess and publish against them". s.52 and s.71 are duties on the
 *    CONTRACTING AUTHORITY, not on the supplier the section is addressed to,
 *    which is a distinction this page already records under CrowMark for
 *    Suppliers. The sentence names the authority as the duty holder instead.
 *    The two duties stay separate everywhere, as they must.
 *
 * C. THE CLOSING SECOND CTA. The render's pair is "Book a 30-minute call" and
 *    "Send a question". The legacy page's closing pair was the call and the
 *    free PPN 002 calculator, and the owner approved keeping the calculator.
 *    The hero keeps the designed pair, so "Send a question" is still on the
 *    page and /contact is still one click away; the close carries the tool.
 *
 * ── MOTION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Three of the five sequences on `245:3` have elements in A2 and are built:
 * M-A1 (hero blooms), M-A3 (role chips), M-A5 (trust dots). M-A2 (a divider
 * drawing down the page) and M-A4 (branch arms, which self-scope to "A6 hero
 * only") have NO corresponding node anywhere in `238:101` — the frame's whole
 * child list is a hero, four role frames, a trust frame and a close frame, and
 * none contains a rule or a stem. Inventing them would be inventing design.
 *
 * Everything here is CSS. M-A5 is specified with an IntersectionObserver, and
 * the build ships 0 KB of JavaScript; a page-scoped observer would be exactly
 * the "one-off JavaScript" PLATFORM-CHARTER.md forbids. A named view timeline
 * does the same job with no script, behind `@supports`, with the final state as
 * the fallback, which is what DESIGN-DECISIONS.md section 3 requires of
 * `animation-timeline`. The trade is honest and is recorded in the report: a
 * scroll timeline is scrubbed rather than played once, so scrolling back up
 * replays it.
 *
 * Every sequence sits inside `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`, so the
 * reduced-motion state is not an override that has to win an argument with the
 * cascade. It is the absence of any animation at all, with every element
 * already at its final state.
 *
 * ── SEO ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The description changed, owner-approved. It read "A London-based team that
 * reads the UK procurement rules", which contradicts the market-neutral
 * decision the rest of this page was corrected to (OA-25). check-seo-parity.js
 * fails on a field that is MISSING, not on one that changed, and /about is not
 * among the routes tests/parity.spec.js discovers — that spec walks blog/,
 * compare/, sectors/ and glossary/ only — so there is no baseline to move.
 * Title and canonical are the legacy values, untouched.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import NewsletterForm from '../components/forms/NewsletterForm.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'About Us | CrowAgent';
/* The overclaim was in here too, which is why it reached og:description,
   twitter:description and the og:image alt text: one string, five places in the
   shipped head. Corrected 2026-08-04, O-07. check-seo-parity.js fails on a
   description that is MISSING, not on one that changed, and /about is not among
   the routes tests/parity.spec.js walks, so there is no baseline to move. */
const description =
  'Written for bid teams and readable by the people scoring them. CrowAgent Ltd: company details, history, what we stand for and the statutes we build against.';

const CALENDLY = 'https://calendly.com/crowagent-platform/30min';
const COMPANIES_HOUSE =
  'https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/17076461';
const PPN_CALCULATOR = '/tools/ppn-002-calculator';
const CONTACT = '/contact#contact-form';

const schema = [
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'About', path: '/about' },
  ]),
];

/**
 * THE FOUR READERS. `mark` is the semantic colour of the artefact the section
 * carries, not a per-section accent — see departure A in the header note.
 * `verified` is a fact cited to a named source; `refused` is the engine
 * declining to write a figure it cannot trace.
 */
type Mark = 'verified' | 'refused';
/*
 * `label` was `chip`, renamed 2026-08-04, and NOT for tidiness. It was named
 * after the CSS class that rendered it, so when styles/labels.css took the name
 * `.chip` for the micro-label pill and this element was renamed `.rolelink`,
 * the rename reached the data binding too: the markup below read `{r.rolelink}`,
 * which is not a field, and the four page-map pills shipped EMPTY. Measured on
 * the served page at 02:07 — four 44px pills with a coloured dot and no words.
 * A content field named after a class name is a field that changes when the
 * stylesheet does. `label` is what it is, not what draws it.
 *
 * It also brings these four strings inside check-english.js, which extracts
 * literals keyed `label`, `heading`, `body` and `eyebrow` and never saw `chip`.
 *
 * THE `eyebrow` FIELD IS GONE. It held the same string as the chip, so every
 * role phrase was printed twice on screen: once in the page map and again at
 * the top of the section it pointed to. The chip is the nav, the h2 is the
 * address, and the artefact still carries the semantic mark, so nothing is lost
 * but the second printing. Approved in specs/ABOUT-PAGE-PROPOSAL.md section 1.
 */
const ROLES: {
  id: string;
  label: string;
  heading: string;
  body: string;
  figure: string;
  source: string;
  mark: Mark;
  /** The artefact sits left and the prose right, alternating down the page. */
  mirror: boolean;
}[] = [
  {
    id: 'role-run',
    label: 'If you run the bid',
    heading: 'You need the answer to survive a year of scrutiny',
    /* Departure B stands: the s.52 and s.71 duties are the AUTHORITY's, not
       this reader's. What changed on 2026-08-04 is that the duties are no
       longer recited here. The artefact beside this paragraph cites s.52 and
       the "How it is built" card states both duties in full; saying them a
       third time in prose addressed to a supplier was the single biggest block
       of the repetition the owner reported. */
    body: 'Everything you price in the response becomes a promise, and the authority publishes at award what it will hold you to. So the draft has to be answerable a year later, not just persuasive on the day.',
    figure: '3 KPIs minimum, published at award',
    source: 'Procurement Act 2023, s.52',
    mark: 'verified',
    mirror: false,
  },
  {
    id: 'role-sign',
    label: 'If you sign it off',
    heading: 'You are the one who has to stand behind the numbers',
    /* THE ONE FULL STATEMENT OF THE CITATION RULE, and it belongs here rather
       than in a block of its own: this is the reader whose whole job it is, and
       the artefact beside it is the rule rather than a figure. Seven other
       statements of it have gone, and what is left elsewhere refers back to
       this one instead of restating it. */
    body: 'A confident paragraph is not evidence. Every figure in a draft is either traced to your own confirmed data and shown with the source it came from, or listed as an open gap. Nothing is rounded into place to close one. That is a shorter review, and a defensible one.',
    figure: 'Every figure, or an open gap',
    // The only artefact on the page that is not a cited figure, because it is
    // not a figure: it is the rule the other three are produced under.
    source: 'No figure without a source',
    mark: 'refused',
    mirror: true,
  },
  {
    id: 'role-score',
    label: 'If you score it',
    heading: 'You are deciding whether to believe a stranger',
    // Refers to the rule stated one section above; does not restate it.
    body: 'The same engine that writes answers reads them. A claim arrives next to the clause it cites, so your check is a comparison rather than a judgement of tone.',
    figure: '10% minimum evaluation weighting',
    source: 'PPN 002, mandatory 1 October 2025',
    mark: 'verified',
    mirror: false,
  },
  {
    id: 'role-deliver',
    label: 'If you have to deliver it',
    heading: 'You inherit somebody else’s promises',
    // "twelve-month" dropped: the artefact beside it says 12 months, and the
    // duty behind that is stated once in "How it is built".
    body: 'The commitments made at bid stage become the KPIs you are measured on. Each one stays attached to the evidence behind it, month by month, so the assessment is assembled from a record rather than reconstructed from memory.',
    figure: '12 months to the assessment',
    source: 'Procurement Act 2023, s.71',
    mark: 'verified',
    mirror: true,
  },
];

/**
 * Two cards. The old pair was "The approach" and "What you get", and they said
 * the same thing twice: both opened on starting from the actual regulation
 * rather than a template. That freed one card for the substantiation the
 * heading needs, and the duplication is merged into the other.
 */
const APPROACH = [
  {
    eyebrow: 'Who builds it',
    /* THE OWNER'S FRAMING, said once and not dressed up. Engineering is what we
       claim about ourselves; bid and consulting experience is what we claim
       about the people who advise us, which is a smaller claim and the true one.
       The dated record that sits behind that advice is in the timeline, once,
       and is not repeated here: it used to be, in full, in both places. */
    body: 'An engineering team builds it, advised by people with bid and consulting experience who have written and won public sector bids. That advice decides what gets built rather than how, and it is why the product starts where the marks are actually lost: evidence the supplier already holds, that nobody can put a hand on in time.',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'How it is built',
    /* THE ONE FULL RECITAL OF s.52 AND s.71 on the page. Everywhere else that
       used to state them now cites them on an artefact or refers to them. */
    body: 'Every feature starts from the source rule rather than a template. PPN 002 for social value, and the two separate KPI duties in the Procurement Act 2023: under s.52 an authority sets and publishes at least 3 KPIs on a contract over £5m, and under s.71 it assesses performance against them at least every twelve months. Each answer carries the clause it was written against.',
  },
];

/**
 * Mission and Vision were both scoped to the UK, which contradicts the
 * market-neutral narrative. UK public is kept as the proof point, in Mission
 * only, so the same sentence is not made twice.
 *
 * THEY WERE ALSO TWO RENDERINGS OF ONE SENTENCE. Both opened on a supplier
 * answering a requirement from evidence it already holds; the words after that
 * clause were the only part that differed. Each now carries only its own half:
 * Mission the access argument, Vision the market statement.
 *
 * Values carries the refusal explicitly. The register to define against
 * advertises a "241% increase in success rates"; not making that class of claim
 * is a position, and a position has to be stated to count. What it no longer
 * does is re-explain the citation mechanism, which is stated in role-sign.
 */
const PRINCIPLES = [
  {
    kicker: 'Mission',
    heading: 'Make bidding answerable, whatever your size',
    body: 'Size and budget should not decide who can answer a requirement properly. UK public procurement is where we prove that, because its duties are published and anyone can check our reading of them against the instrument.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'Vision',
    heading: 'Answers you can defend, first time',
    body: 'A market where a supplier answers a tender requirement from evidence it already holds, without paying a bid-writing consultancy to assemble it.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'Values',
    heading: 'Statute over speculation',
    body: 'We do not quote win rates, ours or yours, and we name no customer, client or project as proof. A claim we cannot trace to a source is not made, on this page or in a draft.',
  },
];

/** `status` is rendered as text, never as opacity alone. See the note above. */
type Status = 'done' | 'now' | 'planned';
const TIMELINE: {
  when: string;
  heading: string;
  body: string;
  status: Status;
  link?: { label: string; href: string };
}[] = [
  {
    /* 2010, not 2021, and the second sentence is load-bearing: it is the
       founders' record, not CrowAgent's, and it names nobody because there is
       nobody to name.
       ── UNTOUCHED ON PURPOSE, 2026-08-04 ──────────────────────────────────
       Three claims in this one paragraph are with the owner and are NOT to be
       reworded until they come back: "inside regulated industries" (no page on
       this site says which), "multi-million pound" (the page's own rule forbids
       naming a project, so the largest claim on it carries the least evidence)
       and "the founders", plural, with nobody named and no Person schema.
       What DID change is that this paragraph is now the only place any of the
       three appears: the provenance card above used to state the same record a
       second time, in full. Softening them here instead of asking would be the
       quiet version of the defect. See OWNER-REGISTER.md and O-07. */
    when: '2010 to 2025',
    heading: 'Founding team experience',
    body: 'Bid and tender work inside regulated industries from 2010, including multi-million pound bids written, won and delivered to completion. That is the founders’ own record before CrowAgent, not a result of the product. It is also where we saw at first hand how much of the friction smaller suppliers face is evidence that exists and cannot be found.',
    status: 'done',
  },
  {
    when: 'Jan to Feb 2026',
    heading: 'Regulatory research',
    /* Was a second full recital of PPN 002's two dates and both KPI sections,
       ending on a third statement of the citation rule. The dates are cited on
       the artefact in role-score and the duties in "How it is built".
       "Before the company existed" closes the gap the proposal flagged as F2/F7:
       this research is dated three months before incorporation and the page did
       not say so. */
    body: 'Deep dive into the statutory frameworks the product is built on, before the company existed: PPN 002 for social value, and the KPI duties in the Procurement Act 2023.',
    status: 'done',
  },
  {
    when: 'March 2026',
    heading: 'CrowAgent founded',
    // The number itself is in the company card beside this column, once. It was
    // printed three times within one screen: here, in the link label, and there.
    body: 'Incorporated as CrowAgent Ltd in England and Wales. Engine prototyping begins.',
    status: 'done',
    link: { label: 'The registration on Companies House', href: COMPANIES_HOUSE },
  },
  {
    when: 'May 2026',
    heading: 'Platform live',
    body: 'CrowMark in live production.',
    status: 'now',
  },
  {
    when: 'Q4 2026',
    heading: 'Regulatory monitor',
    // The word "Live" opened this sentence while the chip beside it said the
    // item is not built. Two signals, one of them wrong. The tense agrees with
    // the status now.
    body: 'A regulatory change feed for UK public procurement, surfacing material updates straight into your dashboard once it ships.',
    status: 'planned',
  },
];

const STATUS_LABEL: Record<Status, string> = {
  done: 'Delivered',
  now: 'Live now',
  planned: 'Planned, not yet built',
};

/*
 * THREE ROWS CAME OUT ON 2026-08-04, and each one was printed twice within a
 * screen of itself:
 *   "Registered name: CrowAgent Ltd"  — the card's own <h2>, immediately above
 *                                       this list, is the words "CrowAgent Ltd".
 *   "Data protection: ICO registered" — the trust row two sections below says
 *                                       "ICO registered. Registered with the UK
 *                                       regulator."
 *   "Data residency: UK and EU"       — the trust row says it as a card of its
 *                                       own. The VALUE is unchanged and
 *                                       owner-confirmed (decision F8); only the
 *                                       second printing has gone.
 * What is left is the legal identity and nothing else: where it is registered,
 * the number, when, and how to reach it.
 */
const COMPANY = [
  { label: 'Registered in', value: 'England and Wales' },
  { label: 'Company number', value: '17076461', mono: true },
  { label: 'Founded', value: 'March 2026' },
];

const VARIANTS = [
  {
    heading: 'CrowMark for Suppliers',
    // s.52 and s.71 are duties on the AUTHORITY, so a supplier feature cannot
    // be described as publishing or assessing under either. What a supplier
    // does is produce the evidence, which is what this says.
    // The two section numbers came out: they are recited in "How it is built"
    // and cited on two artefacts. What a supplier DOES is produce the evidence,
    // which is what this now says without borrowing the authority's duty.
    body: 'Tender discovery from Contracts Finder and Find a Tender, the 10% minimum social-value weighting PPN 002 sets on in-scope central government contracts, PPN 017 AI disclosure, and, after award, the evidence behind each KPI the authority assesses.',
    href: '/crowmark',
    cta: 'See the supplier side',
  },
  {
    heading: 'CrowMark for Buyers',
    body: 'Publish your requirements against a deterministic social value rubric, then locate the evidence for each one across the responses you receive, quoted verbatim or dropped. CrowMark organises; your evaluation panel scores.',
    href: '/crowmark-buyers',
    cta: 'See the buyer side',
  },
];

/**
 * TABLE STAKES. Six rows, and the sixth is the reason the section exists: it
 * says what we do NOT hold. The wording of that row is verbatim from
 * data/footer.ts `trustNote`, minus the asterisk that footnote syntax needs and
 * a card does not. We hold neither SOC 2 nor ISO 27001 certification.
 */
const TRUST: { term: string; detail: string; mark: Mark }[] = [
  { term: 'AES-256 at rest', detail: 'Encrypted where it is stored.', mark: 'verified' },
  { term: 'TLS 1.3 in transit', detail: 'Encrypted while it moves.', mark: 'verified' },
  {
    term: 'UK and EU data residency',
    detail: 'Your documents stay in the region.',
    mark: 'verified',
  },
  {
    term: 'GDPR compliant',
    detail: 'Lawful basis, retention and deletion documented.',
    mark: 'verified',
  },
  { term: 'ICO registered', detail: 'Registered with the UK regulator.', mark: 'verified' },
  {
    term: 'ISO 27001 controls',
    detail: 'We follow ISO 27001 controls. We are not certified yet.',
    mark: 'refused',
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/about" schema={schema}>
  {/*
    HERO. `Section` owns the measure, the padding, the centred head block and
    the gradient capsule eyebrow, so nothing below restates any of them. The
    wrapper exists only to give the two blooms a positioned, isolated box to
    paint inside, underneath the content layer.
  */}
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="hero__mesh" aria-hidden="true">
      <b class="hero__b1"></b>
      <b class="hero__b2"></b>
    </div>

    <Section
      eyebrow="Who we build this for"
      title="Written for bid teams. Readable by the people scoring them."
      standfirst="Four people touch a tender and want four different things from it. The sections below are addressed to each of them in turn."
      id="about-hero"
      as="h1"
    >
      {/*
        The chips are the page map, so they are links rather than labels. That
        is also why they are 44px tall: a control that navigates has to be a
        target, and 44px is the WCAG 2.2 floor this repo holds itself to.
      */}
      <nav class="chips" aria-label="The four readers this page addresses">
        <ul class="chips__list">
          {
            ROLES.map((r) => (
              <li>
                <a class="control rolelink sv-hit" href={`#${r.id}`}>
                  <span class:list={['chip__dot', `chip__dot--${r.mark}`]} aria-hidden="true" />
                  {r.label}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <Button href={CALENDLY} target="_blank">Book a 30-minute call</Button>
        <Button href={CONTACT} variant="secondary">Send a question</Button>
      </div>
    </Section>
  </div>

  {/*
    THE FOUR ROLE SECTIONS. These are the one place on the page that does NOT
    use Section's head block, and deliberately: the design addresses each reader
    directly in a left-anchored editorial column with the artefact beside it,
    and a centred eyebrow-heading-standfirst stack would break the address. The
    centring decision is scoped to the head block and to card content, and this
    is neither. Section still owns the measure, the gutter and the padding.
  */}
  {
    ROLES.map((r) => (
      <Section id={r.id}>
        {/*
          THE MIRROR MODIFIER GOES ON THE GRID, NOT ON THE SECTION, and that is
          not a style preference. Astro scopes a component's CSS with its own
          cid, and `Section`'s root <section> carries SECTION's cid rather than
          this page's, so a `.role--mirror` written here and passed down as a
          class prop compiles to a selector that can never match the element it
          was written for. It rendered in the class list and did nothing.
        */}
        <div class:list={['role__grid', { 'role__grid--mirror': r.mirror }]}>
          {/* NO EYEBROW HERE, and its absence is the fix rather than an
              oversight. It printed `r.eyebrow`, which held the same string as
              the chip that links to this section, so every one of the four role
              phrases appeared twice on screen within a scroll of itself. The
              chip is the page map, this h2 is the address, and the artefact
              beside it still carries the verified/refused mark the eyebrow used
              to colour. Owner item O-07, "so much repeated content". */}
          <div class="role__text">
            <h2 class="role__h">{r.heading}</h2>
            <p class="role__p">{r.body}</p>
          </div>

          {/*
            The artefact is a statement and the source that binds it, which is a
            term and its definition, so it is a <dl>. That is also why it stays
            left: surfaces.css excludes a card holding a list from centring
            automatically, "because a list is read down a left edge". Recorded
            here rather than left to look like a coincidence — the design shows
            it left, and the structural rule is the honest way to get there
            instead of a manual opt-out with no argument behind it.
          */}
          <div class="surface surface--pad role__art">
            <dl class:list={['art', `art--${r.mark}`]}>
              <dt class="art__figure">{r.figure}</dt>
              <dd class="meta art__source">{r.source}</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Section>
    ))
  }

  {/* THE HEADING IS THE LEGACY ONE, RESTORED VERBATIM. It read "Built by
      engineers who read the rules." on the pre-26-July page the owner said they
      liked; the Astro rewrite changed "engineers" to "bid practitioners" and
      that is the overclaim reported in O-07. Going back to it is both the
      correction and the honest sentence, and it keeps check-content-parity.js
      matching on a legacy h1 rather than needing an allow-list entry.

      The section id was `practitioners`, which encoded the claim in the URL
      fragment. Nothing links to it: `grep -rn "about#" astro/src _redirects`
      returns nothing, and no gate names it.

      THE STANDFIRST IS GONE ENTIRELY. It said "draft answers cited to source"
      (the citation rule, stated in role-sign) and "UK public procurement is the
      worked example because its duties are published" (which the Mission card
      four sections down says about the same subject). Rewriting it as the
      builder framing only moved the duplication: the "Who builds it" card
      directly beneath it says exactly that, and one sentence between a heading
      and a card that restates it is the shape of the whole defect. The heading
      introduces the two cards; the cards say it once. */}
  <Section
    eyebrow="Who is behind it"
    title="Built by engineers who read the rules."
    id="who-builds-it"
  >
    <div class="pair">
      {
        APPROACH.map((a) => (
