</Base>

<style>
  /* ══ HERO ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     The two blooms of Figma `240:3` and `240:4`. Same rig as FinalCta and
     HeroStack: absolutely positioned discs painting --orb-teal and
     --orb-violet through `mix-blend-mode: screen`, because normal blending
     would paint two grey discs onto a dark page. They sit at z-index -1 inside
     an isolated context, so no light lands on the surface a word sits on. */
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  .hero__mesh {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .hero__b1,
  .hero__b2 {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(90px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  /* Teal left, violet right, matching the render's ellipse placement.

     SIZED OFF --measure, NOT IN PIXELS. The render's ellipses are 1200 and 954
     wide on a 1440 frame, which is 1.03 and 0.82 of the 1160 measure this site
     lays out on. Writing those as px would put two more container widths on the
     list tokens.css exists to end, and would also stop the light scaling with
     the page. `min()` against a viewport width keeps the same proportion on a
     phone, where 82% of the measure would be wider than the screen. */
  .hero__b1 {
    width: min(78vw, calc(var(--measure) * 1.03));
    aspect-ratio: 1.9;
    left: -8%;
    top: -46%;
    background: var(--orb-teal);
    /* --orb-teal is already 0.78 alpha at its core and this disc is roughly
       eight times the area of the ones on the homepage, so the same alpha reads
       eight times brighter. Measured against the render at 1440, the light has
       to come down to about a third for the hero to stay a dark page with light
       on it rather than a coloured page. */
    opacity: 0.34;
  }

  .hero__b2 {
    width: min(62vw, calc(var(--measure) * 0.82));
    aspect-ratio: 1.6;
    right: -4%;
    top: -42%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
    opacity: 0.26;
  }

  /* ══ ROLE CHIPS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .chips {
    margin-bottom: var(--stack-2);
  }

  .chips__list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* --radius-pill and a 10px vertical padding put this well below the card
     scale, so it is a chip by construction rather than by exemption. 44px is
     the WCAG 2.2 target floor, not a style choice. */
  /* RENAMED FROM `.chip`, 2026-08-04. styles/labels.css now owns `.chip` as the
     one micro-label pill — 13px mono, uppercase — and this is not that: it is a
     44px navigation pill at body size, carrying a sentence, whose height is the
     WCAG 2.2 target floor rather than a type decision. Two different objects had
     one name, and the shared rule was silently uppercasing this one. A genuine
     second treatment, so it gets its own name rather than an exemption. */
  /* The wash, the hairline and the hover are `.control` in styles/controls.css
     (A-52) — this pill and `.art__pill` on the article layout had reached the
     identical two-declaration hover independently, in two files. The ring is
     the sitewide one in styles/motion.css. What stays is the 44px target and
     the type. */
  .rolelink {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 1rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.2;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .chip__dot {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  /* The palette rule, stated once and reused by the artefacts and the trust
     row: teal is a verified fact, orchid is something refused or not held. */
  .chip__dot--verified {
    background: var(--c-verified);
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--c-verified-glow);
  }

  .chip__dot--refused {
    background: var(--c-refused);
  }

  /* Centred at the call site, which is where Section.astro says a body that
     needs centring has to ask for it. */

  /* ══ THE FOUR ROLE SECTIONS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
     520 / 120 / 480 in the render, on a 1440 frame with 160 margins. Expressed
     as a ratio so it holds at every width the measure resolves to. */
  /* ── O-46 · THE TWO COLUMNS SHARE A TOP EDGE, THEY ARE NOT CENTRED ────────
   *
   * WHAT WAS REPORTED. "The left column is top-aligned while the right column's
   * heading and body sit at a different offset — the numbers heading gets
   * clipped against the top of its row."
   *
   * NOTHING IS CLIPPED, and the distinction is worth writing down because the
   * two words describe different defects. Measured on the built page at 1440,
   * 834 and 390 before changing anything: every ancestor of `.role__h` computes
   * `overflow: visible` and no ancestor carries a clip-path, so no ink is lost.
   * The heading's first line box paints 3.0px ABOVE its own border box and the
   * last 2.1px below it — line-height 1.14 is tighter than the font's natural
   * line box — but that ink lands inside the section's 85.5px of top padding and
   * is fully drawn. What the reader is seeing is the OFFSET, not a cut.
   *
   * THE OFFSET IS THE OLD `align-items: center`. The prose column is taller than
   * the artefact card, so centring drops the card by half the difference and the
   * heading is left alone at the top of the row. Measured at 1440:
   *
   *   role-run      prose 297.5 tall, card 187.7  card top 54.9px below the heading
   *   role-sign     prose 327.2 tall, card 148.4  card top 89.4px below
   *   role-score    prose 267.7 tall, card 187.7  card top 40.0px below
   *   role-deliver  prose 327.2 tall, card 187.7  card top 69.8px below
   *
   * The largest of the four is role-sign, which is the "numbers" section the
   * report names — so the report is pointing at the worst instance of a defect
   * that is on all four rows.
   *
   * THE RENDER SETTLES IT rather than taste. In `238:101` all four ROLE frames
   * put the heading text at y=116 and the artefact frame at y=124, on frames of
   * identical height and with columns of unequal content. A CONSTANT 8px, four
   * times over, is a shared top edge; a centre would have moved with the copy.
   * `align-items: center` was a departure from the design that nothing recorded.
   *
   * `start`, NOT `start` PLUS THE RENDER'S 8px. That 8px is a hair less than the
   * 3.0px of ascender this heading already paints above its own box, so writing
   * it here would be a magic number sitting on top of a half-leading that
   * changes with the type scale. The columns share the heading's box top and the
   * optical difference from the render is 5px on a 1440 frame.
   *
   * BELOW 900px THIS RULE IS INERT: the grid is already one column there (see
   * the responsive block at the foot of this file), so the change is a
   * two-column decision and nothing else. Verified at 834 and 390.
   *
   * It does not argue with styles/surfaces.css. The card is the only `.surface`
   * in this grid, so neither half of that file's group-of-cards stretch rule
   * matches it, and a lone card keeps its container's alignment. */
  .role__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.08fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    align-items: start;
  }

  /* Prose stays in source order for a screen reader and for the tab ring; only
     the painted columns swap. Nothing in the artefact card is focusable, so
     there is no reading order to break. */
  .role__grid--mirror .role__text {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1;
  }

  .role__grid--mirror .role__art {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1;
  }

  /* `.role__eyebrow` AND ITS TWO MARKER HUES DELETED, 2026-08-04. The recipe
     had already moved to styles/labels.css that morning; what was left here was
     the two marker colours, and the element they coloured has gone with the
     duplicate role eyebrow (see the markup above). No colour value and no token
     reference changed: two rules for an element that no longer exists were
     removed. The mark itself is unaffected, drawn by `.art--verified::before`
     and `.art--refused::before` on the artefact beside the prose. */

  .role__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h2);
    line-height: 1.14;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .role__p {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* ══ THE ARTEFACT ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     `surface surface--pad` in the markup, so the border, radius, glass,
     specular top edge and cast shadow are the one card recipe. Nothing here
     restates any of them. */
  .art {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The mark. In flow rather than absolutely positioned, so it cannot drift
     away from the figure it belongs to at any width. */
  .art::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: 8px;
    height: 8px;
    margin-bottom: var(--stack-1);
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  .art--verified::before {
    background: var(--c-verified);
    box-shadow: 0 0 12px var(--c-verified-glow);
  }

  .art--refused::before {
    background: var(--c-refused);
  }

  /* --t-h3 rather than the render's ~28px, which is on no tier. The scale has
     four sizes and a fifth opinion is how it starts to drift. */
  .art__figure {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h3);
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .art__source {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* ══ TABLE STAKES ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .trust {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(16px, 2.4vw, 28px);
  }

  .trust__pair {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .trust__term {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .trust__term::before {
    content: '';
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 7px;
    height: 7px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--c-verified);
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--c-verified-glow);
  }

  /* The one row that is not a claim. Orchid, unglowed, and it never moves. */
  .trust__card--refused .trust__term::before {
    background: var(--c-refused);
    box-shadow: none;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  .trust__detail {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* ══ MOTION ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     Every sequence lives inside `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`, so
     reduced motion is not an override that has to win an argument with the
     cascade: it is zero animations, with every element already painted at its
     final state by the rules above.

     Durations are stated as multiples of --m-beat rather than as milliseconds,
     which is the cadence grammar in tokens.css. 1200ms is 2 beats, 260ms is
     0.44, 60ms is 0.1. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* M-A1 · THE HERO LIGHTS FROM BOTH SIDES
       On load, once. Teal first, violet 0.3 of a beat behind it. The render's
       keyframes show both blooms already at 0.55 of final alpha at 0ms and the
       teal reaching full by 400ms of a 1200ms envelope, which is what
       --m-ease-land does on its own: it spends most of its travel early and
       then settles. No breathing loop and no infinite scale, so nothing on
       this page is still moving once it has arrived. */
    .hero__b1,
    .hero__b2 {
      animation: bloom-in calc(var(--m-beat) * 2) var(--m-ease-land) both;
    }

    .hero__b2 {
      animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.3);
    }

    /* M-A3 · THE FOUR ROLE CHIPS ARRIVE IN READING ORDER
       260ms each, 60ms apart, ease-out, after the standfirst. They are a page
       map rather than the argument, so nothing is lost if the animation never
       runs, and the whole sequence is over in 440ms. */
    .chips__list li .rolelink {
      animation: chip-in calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.44) ease-out both;
    }

    .chips__list li:nth-child(2) .rolelink { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.1); }
    .chips__list li:nth-child(3) .rolelink { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.2); }
    .chips__list li:nth-child(4) .rolelink { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.3); }
  }

  @keyframes bloom-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0.55;
      transform: scale(0.94);
    }
  }

  /* THE FLOOR IS 0.35, NOT 0. Corrected 2026-08-03, after check-motion.js
     reported this as a live entrance-from-zero.

     With `both` fill and delays running to 0.3 of a beat, the fourth chip sat
     at opacity 0 for the first 440ms of every page load. Not permanently
     hidden, and reduced-motion readers never saw it because the whole block is
     inside `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference` — but it is the same
     hide-then-reveal shape that failed the axe gate on this codebase before,
     and axe samples DURING an animation.

     0.35 is not a new number: it is the floor styles/motion.css uses for every
     arrival on the site, so the two agree rather than merely coexist.

     STILL DEBT, and recorded as such in the gate: this is a component-local
     entrance, and motion.css now owns arrival. It survives because these chips
     sit above the fold, where the central arrival correctly does nothing, so
     deleting it would remove the sequence rather than centralise it. That is a
     design decision and not a defect fix. */
  @keyframes chip-in {
    from {
      /* 0.75, tracking --arrive-floor in styles/motion.css. It was 0.35, and
         that value was measured as 2.53:1 composited on 2026-08-03. The two
         must agree: a page-local entrance at a laxer floor than the central
         one is the same defect wearing a different class name. */
      opacity: 0.75;
      transform: translateY(12px);
    }
  }

  /* M-A5 · TRUST DOTS SETTLE, AND ONE DOES NOT CELEBRATE
     The sheet specifies an IntersectionObserver at 25%. This build ships 0 KB
     of JavaScript and a page-scoped observer is the one-off script
     PLATFORM-CHARTER.md forbids, so a named view timeline does the same job in
     CSS. `view-timeline-name` goes on the row and each dot reads it, which is
     what gives the six their stagger: the same animation over six ranges
     offset by 5% of the row's entry.

     THE FALLBACK IS THE FINAL STATE, not the start state, which is the rule
     DESIGN-DECISIONS.md section 3 sets for `animation-timeline` while Firefox
     still has it behind a flag. Everything above already paints the settled
     dot; this only takes it away and gives it back.

     The refused dot is excluded by construction rather than by an override. It
     marks the thing we do not hold, and a mark that celebrates its arrival is
     the wrong reading of that. */
  @supports (animation-timeline: --trust-row) {
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .trust {
        view-timeline-name: --trust-row;
        view-timeline-axis: block;
      }

      .trust__card:not(.trust__card--refused) .trust__term::before {
        animation: dot-settle linear both;
        animation-timeline: --trust-row;
        animation-range: entry 12% entry 52%;
      }

      .trust__card:nth-child(2) .trust__term::before { animation-range: entry 17% entry 57%; }
      .trust__card:nth-child(3) .trust__term::before { animation-range: entry 22% entry 62%; }
      .trust__card:nth-child(4) .trust__term::before { animation-range: entry 27% entry 67%; }
      .trust__card:nth-child(5) .trust__term::before { animation-range: entry 32% entry 72%; }
    }
  }

  @keyframes dot-settle {
    from {
      opacity: 0.4;
      box-shadow: none;
    }
  }

  /* ══ SHARED BLOCKS, UNCHANGED ═════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  /* `.kicker` DELETED, 2026-08-04. It was defined five separate times — here,
     /crowmark, /crowmark-buyers, /partners and /pricing — at 0.14em where the
     canonical eyebrow is 0.16em, so five pages drew the same label four
     hundredths of an em apart from every other page on the site. The markup now
     carries `.eyebrow`, which is styles/labels.css. */

  /* ── Two-up cards, used by the provenance pair and the variants ────────── */
  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px);
  }

  /* `surface surface--pad` in the markup, not a fourth copy of the recipe. */

  .pair__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .pair__body {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .pair__body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .pair__body a {
    color: var(--c-interactive);
  }

  .pair__body a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive); outline-offset: 3px; }

  /* ── Principles ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .principles {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(20px, 3vw, 40px);
  }

  /* CARDS NOW, owner 2026-08-03. They were a bordered band with a 2px teal top
     rule, which made them the only content on the page that looked like a
     section divider rather than an object.

     The teal rule is KEPT as an inset top edge rather than a border, because it
     is what separated the three from each other visually and `.surface` gives
     them an all-round hairline that would otherwise flatten the distinction.

     CENTRED, ON THE OWNER'S INSTRUCTION OF 2026-08-03. These were left
     until then, on the argument that a five to seven line paragraph is read
     rather than scanned so every line would start somewhere different. That
     argument was accepted and has been overruled: "all the text in cards or non
     cards must be in central". The opt-out it used, `surface--read`, is retired
     sitewide; see styles/alignment.css. */
  .principles__item {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .principles__item::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0 0 auto 0;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--c-ambient-start);
  }

  .principles__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .principles__body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* ══ HISTORY AND COMPANY, SIDE BY SIDE ═══════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Restored from the pre-26-July page on the owner's instruction. That layout
   * was `grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-24 items-start` with a railed timeline left
   * and a sticky company card right.
   *
   * `items-start` IS THE LOAD-BEARING PART, expressed here as
   * `align-items: start`. Without it the two columns stretch to equal height,
   * the aside becomes as tall as the timeline, and `position: sticky` on a
   * full-height element has nothing to travel within — it silently does
   * nothing. That is the whole trick of the legacy layout and the easiest half
   * of it to drop.
   *
   * SINGLE COLUMN BELOW 1024px, where a sticky sidebar has nowhere to stick and
   * the card belongs after the history it annotates. */
  .hist__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(32px, 5vw, 64px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .hist__grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.15fr) minmax(0, 0.85fr);
      gap: clamp(40px, 4vw, 72px);
    }
  }

  /* Each column titles itself, as the legacy page did — which is why the
     Section wrapping this has no title of its own. */
  .hist__head { margin-bottom: var(--stack-2); }
  /* `.hist__eyebrow` DELETED, 2026-08-04. It is `.eyebrow` in
     styles/labels.css. This was the example the 2026-08-03 audit named: "History"
     rendering as bare mono three sections below a capsule that says "Who we build
     this for", both on this page, both meaning the same thing. */
  .hist__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h2);
    line-height: 1.15;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }
  .hist__title--sm { font-size: var(--t-h3); }

  /*
   * ── THE STICK IS ON THE GRID ITEM, NOT ON A CHILD OF IT ─────────────────
   *
   * `position: sticky` was on `.hist__sticky`, one level INSIDE `.hist__aside`,
   * and it did nothing at all. Measured on the built page at 1440 on 2026-08-04,
   * before this correction:
   *
   *   .hist__grid   top 4877.2   bottom 6339.5   height 1462.3
   *   .hist__col    top 4877.2   bottom 6339.5   height 1462.3
   *   .hist__aside  top 4877.2   bottom 5273.8   height  396.6   position static
   *
   * A sticky box can only travel inside its PARENT's box. `align-items: start`
   * on the grid — the load-bearing part, argued above — makes `.hist__aside`
   * shrink to its content, so the sticky child had 396.6px of parent to move
   * within and 1,065.7px of empty column beside the timeline it was supposed to
   * be annotating. The comment above described the legacy trick correctly and the
   * implementation put the declaration one element too deep, which is a thing no
   * amount of reading catches and one measurement does.
   *
   * The legacy page had it on the grid item itself: `items-start` on the grid,
   * `lg:sticky lg:top-32` on the aside. A grid item with `align-self: start`
   * shrinks to its content while its GRID AREA stays as tall as the row, and that
   * area is what the sticky box travels within. Same two declarations, one level up.
   *
   * ONLY AT 64rem AND ABOVE, where there are two columns. Below that the grid is
   * a single column and the card follows the history it annotates, so there is
   * nothing beside it to stay level with and sticking it would pin a card over
   * the content a reader is scrolling to.
   *
   * `top: 96px` clears the fixed header. The inner `.hist__sticky` wrapper is
   * kept as the block the card sits in; it no longer positions anything.
   */
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .hist__aside {
      position: sticky;
      top: 96px;
    }
  }

  /* ── Timeline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * THE RAIL AND THE DOTS MOVED OUT, 2026-08-04, to styles/timeline.css.
   *
   * They were fifteen lines of geometry that /roadmap needed and could not
   * reach, so /roadmap had written its own — a border on each gapped grid item
   * instead of one on the list, which rendered as four disconnected rails with
   * 60px of nothing between them and no markers at all. This page's version was
   * the correct one; the fix is that it is now the only one, rather than the
   * better of two.
   *
   * The values are unchanged: the same gutter clamp, the same 11px dot, the same
   * arithmetic putting it on the rail. What is left here is what is genuinely
   * about THIS timeline — how far apart the entries sit, where a dot lands
   * inside a padded card, and what each status means.
   */
  .tl {
    gap: var(--stack-2);
    /* Each entry here is a padded card, so its first line starts one card-padding
       down and the marker follows it. The shared default is 0, which is right for
       a bare heading and wrong here. */
    --tl-dot-top: var(--blk-pad);
  }

  .tl__item {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--stack-1) 0;
  }

  /* The live item glows, as it did on the legacy page. */
  .tl__item--now .tl__dot {
    background: var(--c-verified);
    box-shadow: 0 0 14px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-verified) 55%, transparent);
  }
  .tl__item--planned .tl__dot { background: var(--c-border); }

  /* The label recipe is `.meta` in styles/labels.css. What stays is the colour,
     which is a call-site decision and deliberately not part of the shared rule. */
  .tl__when {
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .tl__h {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .tl__p {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .tl__p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .tl__p a { color: var(--c-interactive); }
  .tl__p a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive); outline-offset: 3px; }

  /* The status is a readable chip, so the distinction survives being unable to
     see the colour it is drawn in. */
  /* The pill is `.rolelink` in styles/labels.css — one of eleven geometries measured
     on 2026-08-04, all drawing the same object. What stays is the flex behaviour,
     which is this timeline row's business, and the colour. */
  .tl__status {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .tl__status--now {
    border-color: var(--c-border-glow);
    color: var(--c-verified);
  }

  .tl__status--planned {
    border-color: var(--c-refused);
    color: var(--c-refused);
  }

  /* ── Company details ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* --measure-prose, not the 720px this block used to hardcode. That value was
     on the design-system gate's debt list as "5px away from --measure-prose;
     nobody will ever see the 5px, what they will see is the next value that
     lands 40px away instead". It is a one-line fix and this pass is the edit
     that had it open. */
  /* ── CENTRED AS A BLOCK, ROWS STILL READ LEFT ─────────────────────────────
   *
   * `margin: 0` left this list hugging the left edge of a full-measure section
   * while the eyebrow and heading above it were centred: measured at 1440,
   * 188..913 with about 530px of empty page beside it.
   *
   * It was invisible until today because the CTA card-ification had wrapped this
   * list in a centred card; reverting that, on the owner's instruction,
   * uncovered the underlying block. The card was doing the centring, and the
   * centring was right even though the card was not.
   *
   * The ROWS keep their left edge, because a definition list is read down one.
   * Fourth instance of this same confusion, after /compare, /faq and the PPN 002
   * calculator: where a column sits and how its text ragged-edges are two
   * different decisions. */
  /* THE CARD, not a centred prose column. It used to be a standalone section
     capped at --measure-prose and centred; it is now the right-hand column of
     the history grid, so it fills that column and the width comes from the
     grid rather than from a measure. */
  .company__list { display: grid; }

  .company__row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.2fr);
    gap: 0 1rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-border);
  }

  .company__row:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }

  .company dt {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .company dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── A-02: THE VALUES RIGHT-ALIGN, AND THIS IS A LOCAL DECISION ───────────
   *
   * The label column ends where its longest label ends and the value column
   * began at a fixed x, so four values of different lengths left a ragged right
   * edge inside a card whose own edge is straight. The live site at :8092 sets
   * these against the label as a two-column ledger and the owner considers that
   * correct here.
   *
   * WHY IT IS NOT A SITEWIDE RULE, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH WRITING DOWN.
   * styles/alignment.css names `dl` as a structure that keeps its own alignment,
   * so a definition list is already outside the sitewide centring rule — which
   * means this rule is REACHABLE without arguing with anything, and also that it
   * cannot be hoisted without changing every other list it would then reach.
   * Measured on the built site, there are three other kinds: `.rt__tally` on the
   * homepage is a three-column figure row where each dd is a numeral sitting
   * under its own dt; `.art` and `.trust__pair` on this page are stacked pairs
   * where the dd is a SOURCE line directly beneath the claim it supports.
   * Right-aligning either kind would push a citation away from the figure it
   * belongs to, which is the opposite of what this rule achieves here. A ledger
   * of short scalar values is a different object from a claim and its evidence,
   * and only one of them has a right edge worth lining up.
   *
   * `end` rather than `right`, matching the logical properties used throughout
   * this file, and it is switched off in the same breakpoint that collapses the
   * two columns — a value right-aligned UNDER its own label reads as a second,
   * unrelated column rather than as a pair. */
  .company__row dd {
    text-align: end;
  }

  /* ── The monthly digest card, owner 2026-08-03 ──────────────────────────
     The form sat loose on the page background, which made the page's only lead
     capture the least contained thing on it. Heading and body move inside the
     card with it, so the whole offer is one object. */
  .digest { text-align: center; }
  .digest__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h3);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }
  .digest__p {
    margin: 0 auto var(--stack-2);
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .company dd.is-mono { font-family: var(--font-mono); }
  .company dd a { color: var(--c-interactive); }
  .company dd a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive); outline-offset: 3px; }

  /* ── Responsive ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    .trust { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .principles { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .pair { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .tl__item { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 6px; }

    /* One column, prose first, artefact under it. The mirror is a desktop
       device: at one column an alternating order would put two artefacts back
       to back and read as a fault. */
    .role__grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--stack-2); }
    .role__grid--mirror .role__text,
    .role__grid--mirror .role__art { grid-column: 1; grid-row: auto; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 519px) {
    /* One column, so the ledger stops being one: the value sits under its label
       rather than across from it, and the right edge it was lining up against
       is no longer shared with anything. See A-02 above. */
    .company__row { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 4px; }
    .company__row dd { text-align: start; }
    .trust { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
</style>
